insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('347dfc83-cd59-52e8-a98f-e7d6344e9d47', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 1, 'The Dead End', 'Alder Street ended in a brick wall, a fig tree and a shop that had been a shoe repair, a launderette and a failed vegan bakery, in that order. Jun Park signed the lease on a Tuesday because the rent was cheap and the window caught the morning sun.

The shop opened on a Saturday. By noon, Jun had sold three coffees, two to people who were lost.

The third went to the bookbinder from the shop next door, a man in a cardigan with paper dust on his sleeves, who looked at Jun for a very long moment, said, ''The music is too loud,'' and sat down by the window for four hours.

It was Theo. Of course it was Theo.

Morning sun came through the shop window and lit up the steam from the espresso machine like smoke in a church.

"I''m not here because I like the coffee." Theo paused. "I''m here because the light is good for reading. That''s all."

When Jun opened up the next morning, there was a small hand-bound notebook on the doorstep, with no name inside.', 'published', now() - interval '84.802 days', now() - interval '84.802 days'),
  ('ff87a54f-cd3c-5102-a26d-a799cb0d5243', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 2, 'Too Loud', 'In which Theo comes in every day, complains about the music, and stays for hours. Neither of them mentions the past.

From next door came the steady, gentle thump of Theo''s book press, keeping time like a slow heart.

Jun had spent a decade being sensible. He was beginning to wonder what it had protected him from.

"I''m not here because I like the coffee." Theo paused. "I''m here because the light is good for reading. That''s all."

The fig tree at the end of the street dropped leaves on the doorstep, and Jun swept them up every morning and never minded.

Ten years was long enough to become someone new. Apparently it was not long enough to forget how Theo held a cup.

Grandma Kim arrived with a photo album and a look that meant trouble.', 'published', now() - interval '81.311 days', now() - interval '81.311 days'),
  ('fee770cd-3911-58b9-a1d6-488a250978fb', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 3, 'Grandma Kim', 'In which Jun''s grandmother visits, recognises Theo immediately, and loudly asks if they''re back together. Both men flee to separate rooms.

Morning sun came through the shop window and lit up the steam from the espresso machine like smoke in a church.

Every morning Jun made Theo''s coffee before he arrived. Every morning he pretended it was a coincidence.

Theo said, "You still put too much cinnamon in everything. Some things don''t change."

In the evening the streetlights came on one by one down Alder Street, ending at the shop''s warm window.

Jun had spent a decade being sensible. He was beginning to wonder what it had protected him from.

"I''m not here because I like the coffee. I''m here because the light is good for reading. That''s all," Theo said.

Theo didn''t come in that day, for the first time in three months.', 'published', now() - interval '78.016 days', now() - interval '78.016 days'),
  ('7437ddf1-45fd-596b-ad18-8c1fd3640d9c', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 4, 'Priya', 'In which Priya from the florist across the street becomes Jun''s second regular, and appoints herself narrator of whatever is happening between them.

From next door came the steady, gentle thump of Theo''s book press, keeping time like a slow heart.

Jun had told himself he opened the shop for the cheap rent. He did not look at the bookbindery next door when he thought this.

"Ten years he''s been sad." Grandma Kim paused. "Ten years you''ve been sad. Bah. Make him a better coffee."

The fig tree at the end of the street dropped leaves on the doorstep, and Jun swept them up every morning and never minded.

Every morning Jun made Theo''s coffee before he arrived. Every morning he pretended it was a coincidence.

Theo said, "You still put too much cinnamon in everything. Some things don''t change."

The shop''s first real queue went out the door, and at the very end of it was Theo, pretending not to wait.', 'published', now() - interval '74.658 days', now() - interval '74.658 days'),
  ('ef266e2e-61de-5457-aee7-925ce8a421b6', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 5, 'Cinnamon', 'In which Jun makes Theo''s coffee the way he liked it at seventeen. Theo drinks it, says nothing, and leaves a bookmark on the counter.

Morning sun came through the shop window and lit up the steam from the espresso machine like smoke in a church.

The shop was quiet, and the quiet was slowly becoming the good kind.

