Morning sun came through the shop window and lit up the steam from the espresso machine like smoke in a church.

Jun had told himself he opened the shop for the cheap rent. He did not look at the bookbindery next door when he thought this.

"Ten years he''s been sad. Ten years you''ve been sad. Bah. Make him a better coffee," Grandma Kim said.

In the evening the streetlights came on one by one down Alder Street, ending at the shop''s warm window.

Every morning Jun made Theo''s coffee before he arrived. Every morning he pretended it was a coincidence.

Theo said, "You still put too much cinnamon in everything. Some things don''t change."

The shop''s first real queue went out the door, and at the very end of it was Theo, pretending not to wait.', 'published', now() - interval '24.038 days', now() - interval '24.038 days'),
  ('61424569-f284-51e3-a7b6-fbc1fe205035', '4dd7bf9e-6351-5dfc-a998-683a27007786', 20, 'The End of the Street', 'In which a year after opening, Jun knocks through the wall between the shop and the bookbindery. Theo calls the new space ''too loud'', and stays.

From next door came the steady, gentle thump of Theo''s book press, keeping time like a slow heart.

The shop was quiet, and the quiet was slowly becoming the good kind.

"A shop needs three things: good coffee, a warm window, and someone who keeps coming back," Grandma Kim said.

The fig tree at the end of the street dropped leaves on the doorstep, and Jun swept them up every morning and never minded.

Jun had told himself he opened the shop for the cheap rent. He did not look at the bookbindery next door when he thought this.

Grandma Kim said, "Ten years he''s been sad. Ten years you''ve been sad. Bah. Make him a better coffee."

These days, people walk down Alder Street on purpose. They come for the coffee and the hand-bound notebooks, and the fig tree, and the two men behind the counter who argue gently about cinnamon.

The sign over the door still says Coffee. Underneath it, in smaller letters that Theo painted himself one Sunday morning, it says: & Books. The ampersand is very carefully made. It took him three tries.', 'published', now() - interval '20.982 days', now() - interval '20.982 days')
on conflict (id) do nothing;

