insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('4bb1ae81-4c6d-5e35-a2dd-72c9c9e9831a', '49af270f-d457-5324-a474-492be457e16f', 1, 'Nineteen', 'The glass hearts sat in a case in the great hall of St Aldric''s Academy, on a shelf of black velvet, under a brass plaque that said simply IN MEMORIAM. There were nineteen of them, each the size of a child''s fist, each catching the light from the high windows and throwing it back in small, cold rainbows.

Zara Okafor counted them on her first day, the way you count the steps on an unfamiliar staircase. Nineteen.

That night, unpacking in a dormitory that still smelled of someone else''s perfume, she found a folded note pushed under the mattress. The handwriting was neat and furious. ''They will tell you it was an accident,'' it said. ''Count the hearts. Then count the names.''

Rain streamed down the tall windows of the great hall, and the glass hearts in their case glittered like ice.

Felix said, "Everyone here is lying about something. The trick is working out what it costs them."

When Zara passed the great hall at midnight, the glass case was open, and a twentieth velvet hollow had been cut into the shelf.', 'published', now() - interval '69.653 days', now() - interval '69.653 days'),
  ('817e7436-00ac-5f1c-a0ba-b43c781edbb9', '49af270f-d457-5324-a474-492be457e16f', 2, 'Count the Names', 'In which Zara compares the nineteen glass hearts to the memorial plaque. There are nineteen hearts, but only eighteen names.

Fog lay over the playing fields at dawn, and the old boathouse by the lake was only a dark shape inside it.

Every answer at St Aldric''s seemed to come with a heavy door and a polite smile closing it.

"Everyone here is lying about something. The trick is working out what it costs them," Felix said.

The library smelled of leather, beeswax and old secrets, and its spiral stairs creaked under every footstep.

Everyone at St Aldric''s spoke in a code she didn''t have the key for. She was beginning to learn it word by word.

"St Aldric''s has stood for three hundred years by valuing discretion above all else," Headmistress Crane said quietly.

Her locked diary was on her bed when she returned, open to the last page she had written.', 'published', now() - interval '67.333 days', now() - interval '67.333 days'),
  ('9c8b2e75-120e-500d-a95c-fc50c9f4e391', '49af270f-d457-5324-a474-492be457e16f', 3, 'Felix', 'In which Zara meets Felix Ashworth, a sardonic legacy student who knew the dead girl, Clara, and is the only person willing to say her name aloud.

Rain streamed down the tall windows of the great hall, and the glass hearts in their case glittered like ice.

The girl who had slept in her bed was a name on a plaque now. Zara kept her note folded inside her blazer, over her own heart.

Felix said, "You ask questions like you''ve got nothing to lose. That''s either brave or very stupid."

In the common room, the fire crackled and somebody''s illicit music played too quietly to be caught.

Every answer at St Aldric''s seemed to come with a heavy door and a polite smile closing it.

"Everyone here is lying about something. The trick is working out what it costs them," Felix said.

The Headmistress''s secretary handed Zara an envelope sealed with black wax: an invitation she had not applied for.', 'published', now() - interval '65.139 days', now() - interval '65.139 days'),
  ('89cf190b-1b5f-56c0-a1f3-9beeff7802c8', '49af270f-d457-5324-a474-492be457e16f', 4, 'Clara', 'In which Felix tells Zara that Clara drowned at the boathouse during an ''unofficial tradition'' the school refuses to discuss.

Fog lay over the playing fields at dawn, and the old boathouse by the lake was only a dark shape inside it.

Zara had won her place with a perfect exam score. Nobody had told her the real test began on arrival.

"Scholarship girls never last past second year." Imogen paused. "No offence. It''s just statistics."

The library smelled of leather, beeswax and old secrets, and its spiral stairs creaked under every footstep.

The girl who had slept in her bed was a name on a plaque now. Zara kept her note folded inside her blazer, over her own heart.

