insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('ad44c5b5-5d43-5f94-aaf4-038c4776675d', 'e494bd08-63c6-55cd-aa8a-a20933c60adb', 1, '12:00', 'At 11:58 every night the car park behind Mercado Central was empty except for a parking attendant asleep in his booth. At 12:00 it was full of lanterns.

Lucía Ferrer had been able to see the Night Market since she was eight years old. She had never bought anything there. Her grandmother had made her promise, and her grandmother had been the kind of woman whose promises came with interest.

Tonight she was there in her official capacity. The stallholder who sold bottled weather was lying face-down between his own crates, and the market''s oldest merchant, a man with a moth''s eyes and a banker''s smile, had sent a paper bird to her apartment asking for ''a detective who can see''.

Lanterns of every colour hung over the Night Market''s stalls, and none of them were plugged into anything.

Señor Bram said, "Everything here has a price, detective. The trick is noticing who''s paying it."

When she got home at five, a bottle was waiting on her doorstep, with a small, furious thunderstorm inside.', 'published', now() - interval '3.938 days', now() - interval '3.938 days'),
  ('70a0f5a8-eab4-596f-a973-7a9ced5706e7', 'e494bd08-63c6-55cd-aa8a-a20933c60adb', 2, 'Bottled Weather', 'In which the victim, Tomás, sold bottled weather. His last bottle, a captured hurricane, is missing from his stall.

At four in the morning the market folded itself away in a single breath, leaving only the oil stains and a few stray feathers.

Her grandmother''s warnings came back to her in pieces, like a song she had only half learned.

"Everything here has a price, detective. The trick is noticing who''s paying it," Señor Bram said.

The car park smelled of rain, frying churros and something sharp and electric, like a storm about to break.

Every clue in the market cost something. She was learning to count before she asked.

"Ferrer, you keep closing cases at four in the morning." Inspector Calvo paused. "I''d love to know where you find your witnesses."

When she got home at five, a bottle was waiting on her doorstep, with a small, furious thunderstorm inside.', 'published', now() - interval '3.589 days', now() - interval '3.589 days'),
  ('be70569f-002e-5740-a8e1-14f6cc696eb5', 'e494bd08-63c6-55cd-aa8a-a20933c60adb', 3, 'Señor Bram', 'In which the market''s oldest merchant, Señor Bram, hires Lucía unofficially. Payment: one answer to any question she likes.

Lanterns of every colour hung over the Night Market''s stalls, and none of them were plugged into anything.

Her day job was paperwork and stolen bicycles. Her night job, apparently, was this.

"Your grandmother bought something here once." Señor Bram paused. "Ask her what. I''d love to hear her version."

Rain fell on the city''s tiled rooftops, and the streetlights below turned the wet cobbles into strips of gold.

Her grandmother''s warnings came back to her in pieces, like a song she had only half learned.

"Everything here has a price, detective. The trick is noticing who''s paying it," Señor Bram said.

When she got home at five, a bottle was waiting on her doorstep, with a small, furious thunderstorm inside.', 'published', now() - interval '3.362 days', now() - interval '3.362 days'),
  ('b21d3d2c-a865-5afe-aa52-0791d74bb28a', 'e494bd08-63c6-55cd-aa8a-a20933c60adb', 4, 'Nico', 'In which lucía meets Nico, a young dealer in lost things, who offers to help find the missing hurricane for a price he won''t name yet.

At four in the morning the market folded itself away in a single breath, leaving only the oil stains and a few stray feathers.

Lucía had always kept the Night Market in a separate room in her head, with the door closed. The door was open now.

Nico said, "I sell lost things. Keys, earrings, the name of your first dog. Ask me nicely and I''ll find your killer too."

The car park smelled of rain, frying churros and something sharp and electric, like a storm about to break.

Her day job was paperwork and stolen bicycles. Her night job, apparently, was this.

"Your grandmother bought something here once. Ask her what. I''d love to hear her version," Señor Bram said.

Inspector Calvo left a note on her desk: a photograph of the car park, taken at 12:01, and full of lanterns.', 'published', now() - interval '3.432 days', now() - interval '3.432 days'),
  ('32c24375-a207-5dc6-a8ea-e0dd2dec4e79', 'e494bd08-63c6-55cd-aa8a-a20933c60adb', 5, 'Inspector Calvo', 'In which lucía''s boss, Inspector Calvo, notices she is working strange hours and starts asking questions she can''t answer.

Lanterns of every colour hung over the Night Market''s stalls, and none of them were plugged into anything.

She trusted no one in the market, which was correct, and found she liked several of them, which was not.

"The market looks after its own." Nico paused. "That''s why it''s so scary when it can''t."

Rain fell on the city''s tiled rooftops, and the streetlights below turned the wet cobbles into strips of gold.

Lucía had always kept the Night Market in a separate room in her head, with the door closed. The door was open now.

In the market, every stallholder had turned their lantern blue, which Nico said meant mourning, or war.', 'published', now() - interval '3.148 days', now() - interval '3.148 days'),
