insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('ae823241-7db3-5724-a776-4e8fb6dfae1c', 'cfefaaff-f064-5e08-a2c1-20ae2192cb6d', 1, '01:00', 'Iris Nakamura woke on a narrow bed in a room the size of an ambulance. White walls, a white desk, a white door with no handle on her side. A clock on the wall said 01:00, and it was ticking very loudly.

On the desk lay a card. It was heavy, creamy, expensive paper, like a wedding invitation. In neat black type, it said:

YOU KNEW THE DOSE WAS WRONG.

Iris sat on the edge of the bed and held the card and did not breathe for a long time, because the sentence was true, and she had never told anyone, not even herself, in words.

The house was silent except for the clocks, sixteen of them, all ticking a fraction of a second apart.

"Everyone here has a card." Desmond paused. "Nobody''s sharing theirs. That tells you everything about people."

At the turn of the hour, every clock in the house stopped at once, and then started again, backwards.', 'published', now() - interval '154.898 days', now() - interval '154.898 days'),
  ('3b8ca685-e590-545e-aa14-07e25f85e4d3', 'cfefaaff-f064-5e08-a2c1-20ae2192cb6d', 2, 'The First Door', 'In which at 02:00, Iris''s door unlocks. She finds fifteen other strangers in a central hall, each holding a card they refuse to show.

Air vents breathed softly in the ceiling, cold and faintly sweet, like a hospital at night.

The house didn''t want them dead, she realised. It wanted something worse: it wanted them honest.

"Everyone here has a card. Nobody''s sharing theirs. That tells you everything about people," Desmond said.

White light came from nowhere in particular and cast no shadows, so every room looked like the inside of an eggshell.

The card stayed in her pocket. She could feel its edges against her hip like a second heartbeat.

Down the corridor, someone was knocking from the inside of a room that had already been opened, and emptied.', 'published', now() - interval '148.590 days', now() - interval '148.590 days'),
  ('6585722e-7d5e-5552-af0c-985d93e70905', 'cfefaaff-f064-5e08-a2c1-20ae2192cb6d', 3, 'The Rules', 'In which a calm voice through the vents, the Host, explains: every hour one door unlocks, and the house will open only when every secret is spoken aloud.

The house was silent except for the clocks, sixteen of them, all ticking a fraction of a second apart.

Every person she met was a puzzle and a threat. She kept treating them like patients anyway; it was the only way she knew.

"Trust is a luxury, and this house doesn''t seem to come with luxuries," Desmond said quietly.

Somewhere far off in the house, a door unlocked with a heavy, final click, and everyone held their breath.

The house didn''t want them dead, she realised. It wanted something worse: it wanted them honest.

Desmond said, "Everyone here has a card. Nobody''s sharing theirs. That tells you everything about people."

When she returned to her room, her card had changed. It now held two sentences.', 'published', now() - interval '142.457 days', now() - interval '142.457 days'),
  ('46ddcd84-4830-5f53-a6e8-f0f58d52ee47', 'cfefaaff-f064-5e08-a2c1-20ae2192cb6d', 4, 'Desmond', 'In which Iris meets Desmond, a lawyer who immediately organises the others and refuses to read his own card aloud.

Air vents breathed softly in the ceiling, cold and faintly sweet, like a hospital at night.

Iris had spent ten years making decisions in the back of moving ambulances. She had never had this long to think about one.

"I counted the clocks." Lena paused. "They''re not ticking together. They''re ticking in order."

White light came from nowhere in particular and cast no shadows, so every room looked like the inside of an eggshell.

Every person she met was a puzzle and a threat. She kept treating them like patients anyway; it was the only way she knew.

The fourteenth door opened onto a room exactly like hers, with her own handwriting on the desk.', 'published', now() - interval '136.605 days', now() - interval '136.605 days'),
  ('340fa43e-1e7d-59a9-a813-19228b433757', 'cfefaaff-f064-5e08-a2c1-20ae2192cb6d', 5, 'Lena', 'In which Iris meets Lena, a quiet engineer, who notices the clocks are not synchronised but ticking in sequence, room by room.

