insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('a5f2e7df-6c4e-5f29-a76e-54b31ec618fb', 'e115cf2b-5a7e-5b67-a2a7-cfa3c6c07cc5', 1, 'Hrafnsvík Burns', 'The fire took Hrafnsvík in the hour before dawn, when the dogs were asleep and the men were tired from the feast. Sigrun woke to smoke and her mother''s hand over her mouth, and then her mother pushed her out through the turf wall at the back of the longhouse, into the snow, and did not follow.

She remembered three things from that night. The roar of the burning roof. The cold of the snow on her bare feet. And a man walking calmly between the houses with an axe on his shoulder, its haft wrapped in rings of hammered silver that caught the firelight like teeth.

At her father''s grave she cut her palm and swore. Her grandmother watched her do it and did not stop her. ''Oaths are heavier than iron,'' was all she said. ''You''ll carry it longer.''

The fjord lay grey and still under a low sky, and the mountains rose straight out of the water like the walls of a great hall.

Jarl Eirik said, "Every warrior wants revenge. Few can wait for it. I value the few."

On the far shore, under the jarl''s own banner, a man was sharpening an axe wrapped in rings of silver.', 'published', now() - interval '114.739 days', now() - interval '114.739 days'),
  ('711e1bad-2047-568d-ab4a-52a1d2ccfc0d', 'e115cf2b-5a7e-5b67-a2a7-cfa3c6c07cc5', 2, 'Grandmother Asa', 'In which Sigrun''s grandmother takes her in and teaches her to hold a shield, saying only that an oath must be fed or it will eat you.

In the jarl''s hall the fire roared, and the carved pillars cast long, shifting shadows over the feasting benches.

Her grandmother had said oaths were heavy. She had not said they could also be the only thing holding you upright.

"Every warrior wants revenge. Few can wait for it. I value the few," Jarl Eirik said.

Snow fell on the longhouse roofs, and smoke from the hearth-holes rose in thin, patient threads.

She watched every man with a silver-ringed axe. There were more than she had expected, and none of them were him.

That night, someone cut the ropes of Sigrun''s tent, and left a single silver ring on her pillow.', 'published', now() - interval '111.091 days', now() - interval '111.091 days'),
  ('20434ce4-5071-5fdc-a5b7-8f73825603d1', 'e115cf2b-5a7e-5b67-a2a7-cfa3c6c07cc5', 3, 'Seven Winters', 'In which seven years later, Sigrun is a skilled shieldmaiden who travels to Jarl Eirik''s hall to swear service and gain a place on his ships.

The fjord lay grey and still under a low sky, and the mountains rose straight out of the water like the walls of a great hall.

Her shield arm ached in the cold, a dull reminder of every lesson that had been taught the hard way.

"Loyalty, shieldmaiden, is just revenge that has decided to wait for a better day," Jarl Eirik said.

Ravens circled the battlefield long before the battle, and the old warriors pretended not to notice them.

Her grandmother had said oaths were heavy. She had not said they could also be the only thing holding you upright.

"Every warrior wants revenge." Jarl Eirik paused. "Few can wait for it. I value the few."

The jarl called a council at midnight, and Sigrun was the only shieldmaiden summoned.', 'published', now() - interval '107.140 days', now() - interval '107.140 days'),
