Her shield arm ached in the cold, a dull reminder of every lesson that had been taught the hard way.

Jarl Eirik said, "Loyalty, shieldmaiden, is just revenge that has decided to wait for a better day."

Ravens circled the battlefield long before the battle, and the old warriors pretended not to notice them.

Her grandmother had said oaths were heavy. She had not said they could also be the only thing holding you upright.

"Every warrior wants revenge. Few can wait for it. I value the few," Jarl Eirik said.

A rider came at dusk with news from Hrafnsvík: her grandmother was ill, and asking for her.', 'published', now() - interval '31.442 days', now() - interval '31.442 days'),
  ('7cdd6b7b-ef19-5d79-a0b8-d7fd586582e0', 'e115cf2b-5a7e-5b67-a2a7-cfa3c6c07cc5', 24, 'Ragnvald', 'In which Ragnvald does not trust them. Sigrun proves her word by revealing the time and place of Eirik''s next attack.

In the jarl''s hall the fire roared, and the carved pillars cast long, shifting shadows over the feasting benches.

Sigrun had carried the oath for seven winters. Some mornings she could not remember who she had been before it.

Halvar said, "You fight like you''re arguing with someone who isn''t here."

Snow fell on the longhouse roofs, and smoke from the hearth-holes rose in thin, patient threads.

Her shield arm ached in the cold, a dull reminder of every lesson that had been taught the hard way.

"Loyalty, shieldmaiden, is just revenge that has decided to wait for a better day," Jarl Eirik said quietly.

That night, someone cut the ropes of Sigrun''s tent, and left a single silver ring on her pillow.', 'published', now() - interval '27.844 days', now() - interval '27.844 days'),
  ('4384e6d1-9e77-5fea-a125-a92ab6604cad', 'e115cf2b-5a7e-5b67-a2a7-cfa3c6c07cc5', 25, 'The Trap', 'In which Eirik''s raiders arrive to find Ragnvald''s warriors waiting. Eirik escapes, and knows he has been betrayed.

The fjord lay grey and still under a low sky, and the mountains rose straight out of the water like the walls of a great hall.

The jarl''s favour was a warm fire on a cold night. Sigrun had learned not to sit too close to it.

"When this is over, what will you do with your hands? Have you ever thought about it?" Halvar said.

Ravens circled the battlefield long before the battle, and the old warriors pretended not to notice them.

Sigrun had carried the oath for seven winters. Some mornings she could not remember who she had been before it.

On the far shore, under the jarl''s own banner, a man was sharpening an axe wrapped in rings of silver.', 'published', now() - interval '23.749 days', now() - interval '23.749 days'),
