insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('5ddcb954-f05f-5e60-a5d2-7bbe776cacbb', 'e1cd2a03-2c69-5f07-a242-abe5f81563d1', 1, 'One Point Two Stars', 'The Lucky Kettle had a customer satisfaction rating of 1.2 stars, and Captain Abigail Moss was personally offended by the 0.2. She suspected it was a mistake.

''We deliver,'' she told the man at the docking port, who had a very expensive coat and the kind of face that had never been told no. ''Eventually. Mostly. In one piece, usually.''

''No questions asked,'' said the man, and pushed a crate across the deck. It was the size of a coffin and had several airholes in it. ''Delivery to the Sovereign Palace on Aureline. Double fee on arrival.''

Moss looked at the airholes. The crate sneezed.

''No questions,'' she agreed, and resolved to ask them all the moment he left.

The Lucky Kettle shuddered through hyperspace with a noise like a washing machine full of spanners.

"The engine says it''s tired." Pip-7 paused. "I told it we''re all tired. It said that''s not the same."

On the long-range scanner, three sleek ships dropped out of hyperspace, all bearing the royal crest.', 'published', now() - interval '57.908 days', now() - interval '57.908 days'),
  ('91954899-c050-59be-acea-ca55c1ef6d1f', 'e1cd2a03-2c69-5f07-a242-abe5f81563d1', 2, 'The Crate', 'In which Moss opens the crate and finds Princess Talia of Aureline, who claims she ran away from an arranged marriage and paid to be shipped home in secret.

On the space station''s promenade, neon signs flickered over noodle stalls, dodgy repair shops and courier offices with better ratings.

Every job was supposed to be the one that paid off the debts. Every job somehow made more of them.

"The engine says it''s tired." Pip-7 paused. "I told it we''re all tired. It said that''s not the same."

The galley smelled of burnt toast, engine grease and Gloria''s spores, which were, she insisted, very hygienic.

The crew was strange and loud and constantly broke. She would have fought anyone who tried to take a single one of them.

Gloria announced, calmly, that she had begun to bloom, and that this was probably bad.', 'published', now() - interval '55.411 days', now() - interval '55.411 days'),
  ('fa6326f0-06c1-5e6a-ae5e-57007223f1f6', 'e1cd2a03-2c69-5f07-a242-abe5f81563d1', 3, 'Two Fees', 'In which Moss realises two different clients have paid her: one to deliver Talia to the palace, and one to make sure she never arrives.

The Lucky Kettle shuddered through hyperspace with a noise like a washing machine full of spanners.

Some days she missed the navy: the rules, the clean uniforms, the regular meals. Then she remembered why she''d left.

Captain Moss said, "We are a respectable courier service. We have a rating. The rating is low, but it exists."

The cargo hold was cold and echoing, crammed with crates, half of which were technically not supposed to be there.

Every job was supposed to be the one that paid off the debts. Every job somehow made more of them.

On the long-range scanner, three sleek ships dropped out of hyperspace, all bearing the royal crest.', 'published', now() - interval '53.451 days', now() - interval '53.451 days'),
  ('045775fc-68a0-5716-a05b-05fae7f07668', 'e1cd2a03-2c69-5f07-a242-abe5f81563d1', 4, 'Meet the Crew', 'In which Talia meets the crew: Pip-7, a nervous pilot who talks to the engine, and Gloria, a sentient fungus mechanic with excellent manners.

On the space station''s promenade, neon signs flickered over noodle stalls, dodgy repair shops and courier offices with better ratings.

Moss had spent ten years keeping the Kettle in the air on charm, duct tape and the occasional lie. She was running low on all three.

Gloria said, "I''m a fungus, Captain. I don''t have a stress response. I have a growth response. Please stop making me grow."

The galley smelled of burnt toast, engine grease and Gloria''s spores, which were, she insisted, very hygienic.

Some days she missed the navy: the rules, the clean uniforms, the regular meals. Then she remembered why she''d left.

The engine coughed, sputtered and, for the first time in its life, went completely silent.', 'published', now() - interval '51.092 days', now() - interval '51.092 days'),
  ('b1bbd63d-429c-5d27-a7fb-f6730ada9b31', 'e1cd2a03-2c69-5f07-a242-abe5f81563d1', 5, 'The Royal Fleet', 'In which royal ships arrive searching for the princess. The Kettle hides inside a gas giant''s rings while Pip-7 begs the engine to cooperate.

The Lucky Kettle shuddered through hyperspace with a noise like a washing machine full of spanners.

Moss had a policy about cargo: never get attached. The cargo was currently teaching Pip-7 to play cards, badly.

"Every time someone says ''no questions asked'', a fungus somewhere sighs," Gloria said.

The cargo hold was cold and echoing, crammed with crates, half of which were technically not supposed to be there.

Moss had spent ten years keeping the Kettle in the air on charm, duct tape and the occasional lie. She was running low on all three.

"I''m a fungus, Captain." Gloria paused. "I don''t have a stress response. I have a growth response. Please stop making me grow."

Gloria announced, calmly, that she had begun to bloom, and that this was probably bad.', 'published', now() - interval '48.593 days', now() - interval '48.593 days'),
