insert into public.story_tags (story_id, tag_id) select '18d6bfd3-1cca-557d-aa3e-cb4fe15ec5eb', id from public.tags where slug in ('steampunk', 'inheritance', 'locked-room') on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('6e4227d7-43d3-55e6-a4a3-8d83707c1de0', '18d6bfd3-1cca-557d-aa3e-cb4fe15ec5eb', 1, 'The House That Ticked', 'Ashgrove Hall ticked. It always had. Wren had grown up with the sound of it in the walls: gears turning in the panelling, pendulums swinging behind portraits, the slow brass breathing of the great engine in the cellar that kept every clock in the house in perfect time.

The morning Lord Ashgrove died, the house stopped ticking for exactly one minute. Wren was dusting the library and felt it, the way you feel a heartbeat skip. Then the gears started again, a little faster than before.

By noon, seven relatives had arrived in seven carriages. By evening, the will had been read. And by midnight, Wren was certain of something no one else had noticed: the house was no longer keeping time. It was counting down.

Gears turned behind the panelled walls of Ashgrove Hall, a soft and endless ticking like rain on a far-off roof.

"The house looked after his lordship for forty years." Mrs Hollis paused. "It''ll look after us, if we''re respectful."

At midnight, every clock in the house chimed thirteen.', 'published', now() - interval '159.976 days', now() - interval '159.976 days'),
  ('1a630ed3-31a3-555a-aba6-38c1232152c1', '18d6bfd3-1cca-557d-aa3e-cb4fe15ec5eb', 2, 'The Will', 'In which The will leaves everything to whichever of seven relatives opens the Heart, a brass safe that answers only to its maker.

Rain lashed the conservatory glass, and the mechanical birds in the iron trees tucked their heads beneath their wings.

She was a servant here, and invisible. Invisibility, she was learning, was a kind of key.

"The house looked after his lordship for forty years." Mrs Hollis paused. "It''ll look after us, if we''re respectful."

Gaslight hissed in the corridors, throwing brass reflections across portraits whose eyes really did follow you, on tiny springs.

Seven heirs, one safe, and a house that was counting down. Wren did the arithmetic and did not like the answer.

"A servant''s daughter has no business in the Heart''s chamber." Lady Margery paused. "Know your place, girl."

At midnight, every clock in the house chimed thirteen.', 'published', now() - interval '154.516 days', now() - interval '154.516 days'),
  ('dee62cae-1f82-527f-a771-f5e310670994', '18d6bfd3-1cca-557d-aa3e-cb4fe15ec5eb', 3, 'Seven Heirs', 'In which Wren serves dinner to the seven heirs, from charming nephew Julian to cold Lady Margery, and catalogues their greed in her head.

Gears turned behind the panelled walls of Ashgrove Hall, a soft and endless ticking like rain on a far-off roof.

Lord Ashgrove had taught her to read gears before she could read words. She had never wondered why until now.

"You know this house better than any of us." Julian Ashgrove paused. "Doesn''t that make you the real heir?"

In the dining hall, the automated servers glided between chairs with soup tureens, never spilling a drop, never making a sound.

She was a servant here, and invisible. Invisibility, she was learning, was a kind of key.

"The house looked after his lordship for forty years." Mrs Hollis paused. "It''ll look after us, if we''re respectful."

When Wren returned to the library, one of the bookcases had swung open by itself, revealing a staircase going down.', 'published', now() - interval '149.223 days', now() - interval '149.223 days'),
  ('c61902a4-963a-55fe-a880-c1b44c162bbe', '18d6bfd3-1cca-557d-aa3e-cb4fe15ec5eb', 4, 'Counting Down', 'In which in the cellar, Wren finds the great engine has switched from keeping time to counting down. Seven days remain.

Rain lashed the conservatory glass, and the mechanical birds in the iron trees tucked their heads beneath their wings.

Wren had always thought of the house as a kind of grandfather: strict, strange, and secretly kind.

"My uncle loved puzzles more than people." Julian Ashgrove paused. "I suppose this is his last joke on all of us."

Gaslight hissed in the corridors, throwing brass reflections across portraits whose eyes really did follow you, on tiny springs.

Lord Ashgrove had taught her to read gears before she could read words. She had never wondered why until now.

"You know this house better than any of us." Julian Ashgrove paused. "Doesn''t that make you the real heir?"

An heir was missing at breakfast, and his chair had been carefully pushed in.', 'published', now() - interval '144.302 days', now() - interval '144.302 days'),
