insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('ab1a5413-28db-59c7-a00d-b2a5cb1cf782', '054b855b-86c2-50d2-a240-01ecd72ce88f', 1, 'The Willow', 'The willow was still there. Chioma had been certain it would be gone, taken by the flood or by twenty years of storms, the way everything else had been taken. But there it stood on the bend of the river, older and lower and greener than she remembered, its branches trailing in the water like fingers.

She parked the car at the gate and sat for a long time without getting out. The farmhouse needed paint. The barn needed a roof. The fields on her side of the fence had gone to meadow.

On the other side of the fence, the fields were neatly ploughed, and a tall man in a faded shirt was mending the gate between the two halves of the farm. He looked up at the sound of the car, and stopped.

The river ran slow and brown around the bend, carrying willow leaves and the occasional startled duck.

"I mended the gate every year. Just in case anyone came back to use it," Erik said.

In the attic, behind a loose board, Chioma found a bundle of letters in her father''s handwriting, addressed to Mr Lindgren and never sent.', 'published', now() - interval '94.846 days', now() - interval '94.846 days'),
  ('f4a9abed-0390-5932-af7e-0bd696429529', '054b855b-86c2-50d2-a240-01ecd72ce88f', 2, 'Half a Farm', 'In which Chioma inspects her inheritance: a peeling farmhouse, a roofless barn and fields gone wild. She plans to sell within a month.

Evening light turned the river gold, and swallows skimmed the water for insects in long, looping arcs.

The flood had taken the crops, the barn and both families'' trust. Chioma was starting to wonder which loss had been the worst.

Erik said, "I mended the gate every year. Just in case anyone came back to use it."

Wind moved across the fields in long waves, and the barley bent and rose like the surface of a calm sea.

Every corner of the farmhouse held a memory she had folded away. Unfolding them hurt less than she had expected.

The river rose an inch overnight, and the old farmers at the market started talking about the year of the flood.', 'published', now() - interval '91.599 days', now() - interval '91.599 days'),
  ('17236f60-1fb5-5b6b-ab08-36c979c5edf9', '054b855b-86c2-50d2-a240-01ecd72ce88f', 3, 'Aunt Ngozi', 'In which Aunt Ngozi arrives uninvited to help, bringing yam, opinions and twenty years of family gossip.

The river ran slow and brown around the bend, carrying willow leaves and the occasional startled duck.

Erik had changed and not changed. It was the not-changed parts that kept catching her off guard.

Erik said, "You still walk to the willow first thing. Some things the river doesn''t wash away."

Rain drummed on the barn roof, and the old horses shifted in their stalls and blew softly through their noses.

The flood had taken the crops, the barn and both families'' trust. Chioma was starting to wonder which loss had been the worst.

