insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('4402002f-01c6-5b8d-a44e-5cd2f3393ed3', '29d31a6b-dc05-58af-a39a-133d2a95b699', 1, 'Warm Bone', 'The god under Sorrow''s Reach had been dead for three hundred years, and Kael Vorne had spent fifteen of them inside its ribs with a pick and a lamp and a rope tied around his waist.

God-bone was cold. Everyone knew that. You worked it in thick gloves or it took the skin off your palms, and the marrow inside glowed a pale, sickly blue that made your teeth ache if you looked at it too long.

The new vein was different. Kael found it on the night shift, three hundred yards deeper than any crew had gone, behind a crust of fossilised cartilage. He touched it without gloves, by accident, and snatched his hand back.

The bone was warm. Not warm like stone in sunlight. Warm like a sleeping body.

The god''s ribs curved overhead like the vaults of a cathedral built for something far too large to worship.

"Everyone in this city lives off a corpse." Ysolde paused. "You just dig a little closer to it than most."

That night every marrow lamp in Sorrow''s Reach flickered at once, and in the deep shafts, the bone groaned.', 'published', now() - interval '64.655 days', now() - interval '64.655 days'),
  ('f508b2f7-ee72-5bc6-a567-beee3f8e30c5', '29d31a6b-dc05-58af-a39a-133d2a95b699', 2, 'The Night Crew', 'In which Kael''s crew mines the warm vein. Its marrow is brighter than any found in a century, and the foremen are delighted.

In the miners'' taverns the ale was thin, the songs were old, and nobody ever sat with their back to the floor.

His hand, the one that had touched the warm bone, never felt cold any more, no matter what he did.

Ysolde said, "Everyone in this city lives off a corpse. You just dig a little closer to it than most."

Marrow-light glowed blue in the mine shafts, and every shadow it cast seemed to lean slightly toward the deep.

He dreamed every night now, and every dream was the same: a vast dark, and something in it turning over in its sleep.

"LITTLE ONE." The Dreamer paused. "YOU ARE WARM. COME CLOSER."

In his dream, the dark opened one vast eye, and it had his face reflected in it.', 'published', now() - interval '62.688 days', now() - interval '62.688 days'),
  ('6201f27d-a479-5cc4-ae27-acf301fa68a5', '29d31a6b-dc05-58af-a39a-133d2a95b699', 3, 'Ysolde', 'In which Kael''s old partner Ysolde, now a bone-smuggler, warns him that warm marrow has been showing up on the black market, and its buyers are disappearing.

The god''s ribs curved overhead like the vaults of a cathedral built for something far too large to worship.

Marrow-miners did not live long. Kael had made his peace with that. He had not made his peace with this.

Ysolde said, "If that bone''s warm, Kael, the whole kingdom is standing on something that isn''t finished dying."

A low vibration ran through the bone underfoot, too slow to be a heartbeat, too regular to be anything else.

His hand, the one that had touched the warm bone, never felt cold any more, no matter what he did.

Ysolde said, "Everyone in this city lives off a corpse. You just dig a little closer to it than most."

Kael woke with blue marrow-light glowing faintly under the skin of his palm.', 'published', now() - interval '60.589 days', now() - interval '60.589 days'),
  ('062b8f5a-bb37-54c8-a871-cb7381a4c0c3', '29d31a6b-dc05-58af-a39a-133d2a95b699', 4, 'Dreams', 'In which Kael begins dreaming of a vast darkness and something stirring within it. Every miner who touched the vein dreams the same dream.

In the miners'' taverns the ale was thin, the songs were old, and nobody ever sat with their back to the floor.

Kael had always thought of the god as rock. Since he touched the warm vein, he could not stop thinking of it as a body.

"The gods are dead." Brother Marrow paused. "The Church has said so for three centuries. The Church does not make mistakes."

Marrow-light glowed blue in the mine shafts, and every shadow it cast seemed to lean slightly toward the deep.

Marrow-miners did not live long. Kael had made his peace with that. He had not made his peace with this.

"If that bone''s warm, Kael, the whole kingdom is standing on something that isn''t finished dying," Ysolde said quietly.

Kael woke with blue marrow-light glowing faintly under the skin of his palm.', 'published', now() - interval '58.257 days', now() - interval '58.257 days'),
  ('e9927090-1052-568c-a7bc-43037118c9cf', '29d31a6b-dc05-58af-a39a-133d2a95b699', 5, 'The Humming Miner', 'In which a miner from the night shift climbs out of the deep humming a tune none of them know, and walks calmly off the edge of the city.

